#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433508} :activity/id #uuid "9d89a91b-0f23-5034-8485-cf2fdb766717" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9ce46f-74e5-51fc-85f1-be6a4dc35d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433498} {:answer/id #uuid "144c14ba-18af-5f87-a114-1f0e70bc5c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(-1,-2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433499} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1b9805-9295-5f1f-93ad-1b87d622dbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433500} {:answer/id #uuid "2794a3de-45e3-5d1c-aaa5-e06f453a2bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433501} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eec4e3-df6c-5bff-a85a-74dee074332e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f46da7-c0a4-56d1-b636-f9591428aeed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ffd9297-ce86-5e32-becf-cbd84b607c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-160476" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u-1)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u-1)^{2} & -3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}\\cdot 1-(-3(v-2)^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}=0$ defines the point $(1,2)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "2829a17c-8a17-5054-a69a-9c845f86a147"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "552040e6-e34a-5420-b715-153752988504" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272de190-0e96-5d51-95d4-ff366ae72e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348785} {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} {:answer/id #uuid "93ea44ae-a889-5898-8990-fdfc9599ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349770} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdca57-4e7f-505d-ba98-23b6018b411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350265} {:answer/id #uuid "f22a24d4-0975-5c9c-9994-b66abef9c47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6831b19c-e658-54e3-b4bb-f0560cf60cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a48caee-df37-515d-8b93-7198deb33b28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d467be4-da9c-5949-964e-fc1ece22124b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u+v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402618} {:answer/id #uuid "99d8765a-e6b9-5b6f-b95c-7ee236d3b208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u-v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402619} {:answer/id #uuid "a23b6f7f-749d-5d1a-b619-0c32cdbf023d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} {:answer/id #uuid "128c4bf5-7f24-5412-a170-6a74863be1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402621} {:answer/id #uuid "72a4bc7a-67e1-58b0-9c23-038668c40386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05d1ca1-306f-5d0c-970a-e108a73f246e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48feab89-768b-5b8e-8c0a-02f86fdff070" :question/math-academy-id "q-38245" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -8uv & =0 \\\\ uv & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $uv=0$ defines the pair of intersecting lines $u=0$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane. Notice that these lines correspond to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "df267d9e-6bbe-5490-b116-b3284eaeaf33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd60f7-0060-5908-af95-4c108c3b1718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404165} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5de86-0281-5ef0-9bac-c48c24b2a6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404167} {:answer/id #uuid "90292b65-61da-58e0-bd75-eda1b58d756e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404168} {:answer/id #uuid "22f6e316-1aa7-5c12-abf8-9b28b8e93f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87074f0-9d22-553d-826e-78e5774760d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59e9bc67-c967-5c58-a234-d26582651fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81279" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "e590a33c-a8f9-5732-980b-cf8843af4f45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433507}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433497}]}
